  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy employs a gradient descent optimization method with projection to minimize the overlap integral C\u2085. By focusing on the maximum convolution value across all shifts, the algorithm iteratively refines the step function h to reduce this maximum, ensuring feasibility through constraint projection.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 \u211d^n, with 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h = n/2, where n = len(h).\n- Objective: Minimize max_k [ C(k) ] * dx, where C(k) = \u2211_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i](1 - h[(i+k) mod n]).\n- Constraints: h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] and \u2211h[i] = n/2.\n- The projection ensures feasibility after each gradient step.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialize h using initial_h_values or a plateau function scaled to meet constraints.\n2. Compute cross-correlation to find current maximum shift k_max.\n3. Calculate gradient based on k_max: \u2207h[j] = 1 - h[(j + k_max) mod n] - h[(j - k_max) mod n].\n4. Update h with gradient descent, then project onto feasible set [0,1]^n with sum n/2.\n5. Track best solution and adjust learning rate with backtracking on stagnation.\n6. Terminate upon time budget exhaustion or convergence.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Start with plateau initialization if initial_h_values is unavailable.\n- Use learning rate 0.01 with decay on stagnation.\n- Projection method: clip and scale iteratively to meet sum constraint.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nExpected runtime per iteration is O(n^2), with n=160. The algorithm runs up to 10000 iterations within 1100s, ensuring flexibility to adapt learning rates and escape local minima.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nThe final h is validated to ensure feasibility and compute C\u2085, confirming it meets constraints and providing a concrete bound improvement.",
